A BetterBash tree with this file in it has been fetched but not installed.

The fetch commands of the WebUI leave a tree in ~/.bb itself - one level, the
directory the command names. Nothing reads or runs anything from that directory
until it is asked to: sourcing prompt/bb.sh from inside the tree installs it and
deletes this file, so the second sourcing of that file only builds the prompt. The
install writes no directory of its own either; its files land in ~/.bb next to the
`prompt/` and `installbb.sh` they were copied out of. Installing the tree later is
only that:

    . ~/.bb/prompt/bb.sh          # keeps the theme of this machine
    . ~/.bb/prompt/bb.sh rand     # draws a new theme here

Answered the question with n? Nothing was installed and nothing will be. The tree
is a directory of files, readable, and it changes no shell until someone sources
it; a shell that starts later only mentions it, it does not install it. Running
installbb.sh of the tree directly (a script, a container) installs it too, and takes
this file away as well.

To remove a tree that was fetched and left alone:

    rm -rf ~/.bb

which is the whole of BetterBash on a machine that never installed one. On a
machine that did, ~/.bb holds the installed prompt as well, and removing it removes
both; `sh ~/.bb/removebb.sh` takes the ~/.bashrc and ~/.inputrc blocks with it.
